    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "When the regular pentagon is reflected in the line $PQ$, and then rotated *clockwise* 144° about the centre of the pentagon, its position is",
    "image": "qimages/cemc/fermat-2000/q11.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": ""
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Track the shaded triangle",
        "body": "The pentagon is split into $5$ triangles from its centre; each central angle is $72^\\circ$, so a $144^\\circ$ rotation moves each triangle two places.\n\nIn the original, the shaded triangle is the upper-left one (between the top vertex and the upper-left vertex). Reflecting in $PQ$ turns the pentagon upside down, with side $PQ$ now on top; the shaded triangle becomes the lower-left one.\n\nGoing clockwise around the reflected pentagon the triangles are: top, upper-right, lower-right, lower-left, upper-left. Two places clockwise from lower-left is upper-left, then top. So the shaded triangle ends up as the top triangle, against $PQ$.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
