    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "A circle is tangent to three sides of a rectangle having side lengths 2 and 4 as shown. A diagonal of the rectangle intersects the circle at points $A$ and $B$. The length of $AB$ is",
    "image": "qimages/cemc/fermat-2000/q23.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\sqrt5$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\sqrt5-\\frac15$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\sqrt5-\\frac16$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\frac{5\\sqrt5}{6}$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Coordinates",
        "body": "Put the rectangle with corners $(0,0)$ and $(4,2)$. The circle touches the left, top and bottom sides, so it has radius $1$ and centre $(1,1)$. The diagonal is $y=\\frac x2$.\n\nSubstitute: $(x-1)^2+\\left(\\frac x2-1\\right)^2=1$ gives $\\frac54x^2-3x+1=0$, i.e. $5x^2-12x+4=0$, so $x=\\frac25$ or $x=2$.\n\nThe two points differ by $\\Delta x=\\frac85$ along a line of slope $\\frac12$, so $AB=\\frac85\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}=\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$.\n\n**Answer: (B) $\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Distance from centre to chord",
        "body": "The centre $(1,1)$ is at distance $\\frac{|1-2\\cdot1|}{\\sqrt{1^2+2^2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}$ from the line $x-2y=0$. Half the chord is $\\sqrt{1-\\frac15}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}$, so $AB=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt5}=\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$.\n\n**Answer: (B) $\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$**"
      }
    ],
