  {
    "id": "cemc-fermat-2000-25",
    "bank": "cemc",
    "source": {
      "contest": "Fermat Contest (CEMC)",
      "paper": "2000 Fermat Contest (Grade 11)",
      "year": 2000,
      "number": 25,
      "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/2000/2000FermatContest.pdf"
    },
    "grade": "11",
    "points": 8,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The given cube is cut into four pieces by two planes. The first plane is parallel to face $ABCD$ and passes through the midpoint of edge $BG$. The second plane passes through the midpoints of edges $AB$, $AD$, $HE$, and $GH$. Determine the ratio of the volumes of the smallest and largest of the four pieces.",
    "image": "qimages/cemc/fermat-2000/q25.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "3:8"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "7:24"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "7:25"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "7:17"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "5:11"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Coordinates",
        "body": "Let the cube have side $2$ with $G=(0,0,0)$, $H=(2,0,0)$, $E=(2,2,0)$, $F=(0,2,0)$ on the bottom and $B=(0,0,2)$, $C=(2,0,2)$, $D=(2,2,2)$, $A=(0,2,2)$ on top. The first plane is $z=1$.\n\nThe midpoints of $AB$, $AD$, $HE$, $GH$ are $(0,1,2)$, $(1,2,2)$, $(2,1,0)$, $(1,0,0)$, all satisfying $x-y+z=1$: that is the second plane.\n\nTop half ($1\\le z\\le2$): the cross-section at height $z$ is the $2\\times2$ square cut by the line $x-y=1-z$ (between $-1$ and $0$). Writing $s=1-z\\in[-1,0]$, the part with $x-y<s$ is a right isosceles triangle at the corner $(x,y)=(0,2)$ (below $A$) with legs $2+s$, so its area is $\\frac{(2+s)^2}{2}$. Its volume is $\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{(2+s)^2}{2}\\,ds=\\frac{2^3-1^3}{6}=\\frac76$, and the rest of the top half is $4-\\frac76=\\frac{17}{6}$.\n\nBy symmetry (the map $(x,y,z)\\mapsto(2-x,2-y,2-z)$ fixes both planes), the bottom half splits the same way. The four pieces are $\\frac76,\\frac{17}6,\\frac76,\\frac{17}6$, so smallest : largest $=7:17$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 7:17**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Frustum of a pyramid",
        "body": "In the top half the small piece is the one at corner $A$: it is bounded by the top face, the plane $z=1$, the two faces through $A$, and the slanted plane. Its cross-sections are right isosceles triangles whose legs grow linearly from $1$ (at the top, the triangle cut off by the midpoints of $AB$ and $AD$) to $2$ (at $z=1$). So it is a frustum of a triangular pyramid with end areas $\\frac12$ and $2$ and height $1$:\n$$V=\\frac h3\\left(A_1+A_2+\\sqrt{A_1A_2}\\right)=\\frac13\\left(\\frac12+2+1\\right)=\\frac76.$$\nThe other top piece is $4-\\frac76=\\frac{17}{6}$; the bottom half is congruent by symmetry. Ratio $7:17$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 7:17**"
      }
    ],
